ility of \\(k\\ge2\\) yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) and replace the line \\(y=n\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-n=(1-n)(x-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); the unique point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \nConsequently all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_n\\); each must lie on some line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at mo