eq1\\), the line would be horizontal, contradicting sunniness. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma.* A sunny line can contain **at most one** point whose second coordinate equals \\(n-1\\).  \n   *Proof.* Two distinct points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on a horizontal line, which is not sunny. ∎  \n\n   Consequently, in any family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) the points  \n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1)\n   \\]\n   must be covered by **different** lines, and each of those two lines can be sunny at most once.  \n\n   Suppose, for contradiction, that two distinct sunny lines occur.  \n   One of them must contain \\(P_1\\); by Lemma 1 it cannot contain any other point with \\(y=n\\).  \n   The other sunny line must contain \\(P_2\\); by Lemma 2 it cannot contain any other point with \\(y=n-1\\).  \n\n   All remaining points of \\(S_n\\) have second coordinate at most \\(n-2\\).  \n   Any line that is not horizontal can intersect the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\le n-2\\}\\) in at most one point for each fixed \\(b\\); therefore to cover **all** points with second coordinate \\(\\le n-2\\) we need at least \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines (one for each value \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\)).  \n\n   Adding the two sunny lines gives a total of at least \\((n-2)+2=n\\) lines, which is permissible, **but** the two sunny lines already use up the two “slots’’ for the rows \\(b=n\\) and \\(b=n-1\\).  \n   The row \\(b=n-1\\) contains the two points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\).  \n   The sunny line that contains \\((1,n-1)\\) cannot contain \\((2,n-1)\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal), so \\((2,n-1)\\) would have to be covered by a horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).  \n   That horizontal line would be an **additional** line beyond the \\(n-2\\) horizontals already required, raising the total to at least \\(n+1\\), a contradiction.  \n\n   Hence a configuration with two or more sunny lines cannot exist. ∎  \n\n   Combining the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) with the impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) and replace the line \\(y=n\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-n=(1-n)(x-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); the unique point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \nConsequently all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_n\\); each must lie on some line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point whose second coordinate equals \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((a,n)\\) with \\(a\\neq1\\), the line would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nHence exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) contains \\(P_1\\); that line may be sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most one point whose second coordinate equals \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on a horizontal line, which is not sunny. ∎\n\nConsequently the point \\(P_2\\) must lie on a line different from the one containing \\(P_1\\); that line may also be sunny.\n\nNow consider the row \\(y=n-1\\).  \nIt consists of the two points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\).  \nThe line that contains \\((1,n-1)\\) cannot contain \\((2,n-1)\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \nTherefore \\((2,n-1)\\) must be covered by a **horizontal** line \\(y=n-1\\), which is non‑sunny.\n\nThus, besides the two (possibly sunny) lines that contain \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\), we necessarily need the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).\n\nFor every integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nH_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least one point and all its points share the same second coordinate \\(b\\).  \nIf a line is not horizontal, it can intersect the set \\(H_b\\) in **at most one** point (otherwise two points of \\(H_b\\) would lie on a non‑horizontal line, impossible).  \nHence to cover all points with second coordinate \\(b\\) we need a distinct horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nTherefore the family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) must contain the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-2,\\;y=n-1,\n\\]\n\nthat is, \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines.  \nAdding the two lines that contain \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) gives at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+2 = n+1\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nHence a configuration with two or more sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe constructions of Sections 1 and 2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSection 3 proves that \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
