nd the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – The answer remains unchanged: for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \n1. **Existence** –  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* is realised by the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* is realised by the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)** –  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most one point whose second coordinate equals \\(n\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A sunny line contains at most one point whose second coordinate equals \\(n-1\\).  \n   *Lemma 3.* For every \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) the set of points with second coordinate \\(b\\) (the “row’’ \\(y=b\\)) cannot be covered by fewer than one non‑horizontal line; consequently at least one **horizontal** line is needed for each such \\(b\\).  \n\n   Using the lemmas we perform a case‑analysis on how the two highest rows \\(y=n\\) and \\(y=n-1\\) are covered. In every possible arrangement we are forced to use at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available. Hence a configuration with two or more sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nThe detailed proof below fills the gaps pointed out in the previous attack (in particular the role of a possible vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the correct counting of required horizontal lines).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny.  If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Keep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and replace the line \\(y=n\\) by  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-n=(1-n)(x-1),\n  \\]\n\n  the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Its slope is \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on the corresponding horizontal line, while the unique point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\nConsequently \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemmas needed for the impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1),\\qquad Q=(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) belong to \\(S_n\\); the row \\(y=n-1\\) consists precisely of the two points \\(P_2\\) and \\(Q\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point whose second coordinate equals \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((a,n)\\) with \\(a\\neq1\\), the line would be horizontal, contradicting sunniness. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point whose second coordinate equals \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on a horizontal line, which is not sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\n(the row \\(y=b\\)) cannot be covered by fewer than one **horizontal** line.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(R_b\\) share the same second coordinate \\(b\\).  \nIf a non‑horizontal line (i.e. a line whose slope is not \\(0\\)) contained two distinct points of \\(R_b\\), those two points would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be horizontal – a contradiction. Hence each non‑horizontal line meets \\(R_b\\) in at most one point, and at least \\(|R_b|\\) distinct non‑horizontal lines would be required to cover the whole row. Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, the only feasible way to cover \\(R_b\\) is to use a **horizontal** line \\(y=b\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least two sunny lines.  \nWe distinguish two exhaustive cases according to whether the vertical line \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case A – The line \\(x=1\\) is **not** in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nBecause \\(x=1\\) is absent, the points \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) must lie on **different** lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 the line that contains \\(P_1\\) cannot contain any other point with second coordinate \\(n\\); therefore that line is the **only** line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n\\).  \nSimilarly, Lemma 2 shows that the line containing \\(P_2\\) is the only line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n-1\\) at a point other than \\(Q\\).\n\nThe point \\(Q\\) still has to be covered. It cannot lie on the line through \\(P_2\\) (otherwise that line would be 