ongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case A – The line \\(x=1\\) is **not** in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nBecause \\(x=1\\) is absent, the points \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) must lie on **different** lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 the line that contains \\(P_1\\) cannot contain any other point with second coordinate \\(n\\); therefore that line is the **only** line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n\\).  \nSimilarly, Lemma 2 shows that the line containing \\(P_2\\) is the only line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n-1\\) at a point other than \\(Q\\).\n\nThe point \\(Q\\) still has to be covered. It cannot lie on the line through \\(P_2\\) (otherwise that line would be horizontal), so \\(Q\\) must be covered by a **horizontal** line \\(y=n-1\\). Consequently the row \\(y=n-1\\) uses **two** distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\): one (possibly sunny) passing through \\(P_2\\) and the horizontal line covering \\(Q\\).\n\nNow apply Lemma 3 to each row \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\).  \nEach such row forces the presence of the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain at least\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-2)}_{\\text{horizontal rows }y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{rows }y=n,\\;y=n-1}\n\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nBut we have already used **two** sunny lines (the ones through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\)).  \nIf either of those sunny lines coincides with one of the required horizontals, it would be horizontal and therefore not sunny – a contradiction.  \nThus the two sunny lines are **additional** to the \\(n\\) lines just counted, giving at least \\(n+2\\) lines in total, which exceeds the allowed number \\(n\\).  \nHence Case A is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B – The vertical line \\(x=1\\) **is** in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nThe line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) covers both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \nThe remaining point of the row \\(y=n-1\\), namely \\(Q=(2,n-1)\\), must be covered by a line different from \\(x=1\\).  \nBecause a non‑horizontal line can meet the row \\(y=n-1\\) in at most one point, the only way to cover \\(Q\\) without using a horizontal line is to let a **sunny** line pass through it.\n\nThus at least one sunny line is forced to contain \\(Q\\).  \nIf a second sunny line existed, it would have to cover some other point of \\(S_n\\).  \nConsider the rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).  \nBy Lemma 3 each of these rows requires a horizontal line, and none of those horizontals can be sunny.  \nTherefore the total number of lines needed is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-2)}_{\\text{horizontal rows }y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{x=1}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{sunny line through }Q}\n\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\nif there is **exactly one** sunny line.  \nIf we attempted to introduce a second sunny line, we would need an extra line beyond the \\(n\\) already accounted for, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(|\\mathcal F|=n\\).\n\nHence a configuration containing two or more sunny lines cannot exist in Case B either.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince both exhaustive cases lead to contradictions, a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) can contain **at most one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe constructions of Section 1 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSection 3 proves that \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
