can meet the row \\(y=n-1\\) in at most one point, the only way to cover \\(Q\\) without using a horizontal line is to let a **sunny** line pass through it. Thus at least one sunny line is forced.\n\nNow we have exactly one vertical line, at least one sunny line, and the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑vertical.  \nApply Lemma 3 to the lower rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\).  \nThe number of non‑horizontal, non‑vertical lines available is at most the number of sunny lines, say \\(s\\).  \nTherefore at most \\(s\\) of the rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\) can be left without a horizontal line; the other \\((n-2)-s\\) rows each require their own horizontal line.\n\nCounting distinct lines we obtain at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1}_{x=1}+\n\\underbrace{s}_{\\text{sunny lines}}+\n\\underbrace{(n-2)-s}_{\\text{mandatory horizontals}}=\nn-1\n\\]\n\nlines.  If \\(s\\ge2\\) (i.e. at least two sunny lines) the term \\((n-2)-s\\) is at most \\(n-4\\), and the total becomes  \n\n\\[\n1+s+(n-2-s)=n-1,\n\\]\n\nbut we have **still** to cover the point \\((1,n)\\) which lies on the vertical line already, and the point \\((1,n-1)\\) as well; the only uncovered point is \\((2,n-1)=Q\\), already covered by a sunny line.  \nThus the count \\(n-1\\) would suggest we have one spare line, but recall that the sunny line through \\(Q\\) also meets some lower row (because its slope is not \\(-1,0,\\infty\\)). That meeting consumes the *only* non‑horizontal line that could have served for another lower row, so one of the rows that we counted as “mandatory horizontal’’ would actually need a second line, pushing the total to at least \\(n\\!+\\!1\\). A more transparent way is to observe that the \\(s\\) sunny lines already occupy the only \\(s\\) non‑horizontal slots allowed by Lemma 3; consequently **all** lower rows must be covered by horizontal lines, giving exactly \\(n-2\\) horizontals. Adding the vertical line and the \\(s\\ge2\\) sunny lines yields at least  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-2)+s\\ge n+1\n\