fies the earlier gap concerning Lemma 3.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny.  If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Keep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and replace the line \\(y=n\\) by  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-n=(1-n)(x-1),\n  \\]\n\n  the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Its slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on the corresponding horizontal line, while the unique point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\nConsequently \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemmas needed for the impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1),\\qquad Q=(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) belong to \\(S_n\\); the row \\(y=n-1\\) consists precisely of the two points \\(P_2\\) and \\(Q\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point whose second coordinate equals \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((a,n)\\) with \\(a\\neq1\\), the line would be horizontal, contradicting sunniness. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains **at most one** point whose second coordinate equals \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on a horizontal line, which is not sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (re‑formulated)  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\) let  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line is **non‑horizontal**, it meets \\(R_b\\) in **at most one** point.  \nHence, if a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of lines contains at most \\(t\\) non‑horizontal lines, then at most \\(t\\) of the rows \\(\\{R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\}\\) can be covered without using a horizontal line; every other such row must be covered by its own horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(R_b\\) share the same second coordinate \\(b\\).  Two distinct points of \\(R_b\\) would force any line containing them to be horizontal; therefore a non‑horizontal line can intersect \\(R_b\\) in at most one point.  If a row \\(R_b\\) is not covered by a horizontal line, each of its points must lie on a distinct non‑horizontal line.  Consequently the number of rows that can avoid a horizontal line is bounded by the total number of non‑horizontal lines available, i.e. by \\(t\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least two sunny lines.  \nLet \\(s\\ge2\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nAll other lines are non‑sunny; among them there may be vertical lines, horizontal lines, or lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nWe distinguish two exhaustive cases according to whether the vertical line \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case A – The line \\(x=1\\) is **not** in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nBecause \\(x=1\\) is absent, the points \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) must lie on **different** lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 the line containing \\(P_1\\) is the **only** line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n\\); by Lemma 2 the line containing \\(P_2\\) is the **only** line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that meets the row \\(y=n-1\\) at a point other than \\(Q\\).\n\nThe point \\(Q\\) still has to be covered. It cannot lie on the line through \\(P_2\\) (otherwise that line would be horizontal), so \\(Q\\) must be covered by a **horizontal** line \\(y=n-1\\).  \nThus the two top rows require **three** distinct lines:\n\n* one (possibly sunny) line through \\(P_1\\);\n* one (possibly sunny) line through \\(P_2\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\) covering \\(Q\\).\n\nNow consider the lower rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\).  \nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) we have at most \\(s\\) sunny lines and at most one vertical line (the latter is absent in this case). Hence the total number of **non‑horizontal, non‑vertical** lines is at most \\(s\\).  \nApplying Lemma 3 with \\(t=s\\), at most \\(s\\) of the rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\) can avoid a horizontal line.  \nSince \\(s\\ge2\\) and there are \\(n-2\\) such rows, at least \\((n-2)-s\\) of them must be covered by their own horizontal lines.\n\nConsequently the total number of distinct lines needed is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{top rows}}+\n\\underbrace{(n-2)-s}_{\\text{mandatory horizontals in lower rows}}+\n\\underbrace{s}_{\\text{the sunny lines themselves}}\n= n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThis exceeds the allowed number \\(n\\), a contradiction. Hence Case A cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B – The vertical line \\(x=1\\) **is** in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nThe line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) covers both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \nThe remaining point of the row \\(y=n-1\\), namely \\(Q\\), must be covered by a line different from \\(x=1\\).  \nBecause a non‑horizontal line